{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtNtn2q98vsgwbyWu3ZF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added a `codex-sandboxed` lane: `./run.sh <task> codex-sandboxed`. I haven't done a real end-to-end run, because `CODEX_API_KEY` isn't set here and a run would send the prompt to OpenAI. I did test the sandbox fence itself (results below).\n\nThe lane runs the same `codex exec` command as the `codex` lane, wrapped the way `kimi` is: inside `sandbox-exec` with `sandbox/agent.sb`, and with HOME and `CODEX_HOME` pointing at an empty scratch folder in `/tmp` that's deleted afterwards. Codex logs in with `CODEX_API_KEY`, so nothing in your real home is opened for it, not even `~/.codex`.\n\nTwo problems came up with copying the kimi lane directly, and I fixed both:\n- **`agent.sb` always opened `~/.kimi-code`.** Reused as it was, Codex's shell could have read Kimi's credentials and sessions. The folder the profile opens is now a parameter (`-D AGENT_DIR=...`). The kimi lane passes `~/.kimi-code`, so it has the same access as before; the codex lane passes its scratch folder.\n- **`final.txt` would have been blocked.** Codex writes its last message (`-o`) from inside the sandbox, and on the eval Mac the repo is in `~/evals/...`, inside the home the profile blocks. The lane writes it to the scratch folder and copies it into the run folder afterwards.\n\nCodex's own sandbox stays turned off, as in the `codex` lane: it uses the same macOS mechanism, and the two can't be nested.\n\n**Fence test:** I ran the lane's exact sandbox setup with a test script in place of the agent.\n- **Codex lane settings:** reading `~/.codex/auth.json`, reading `~/.kimi-code` and writing anywhere in `~` were all blocked. Writing to the scratch folder and the work folder, `codex --version` and `timeout` all worked.\n- **Kimi lane settings:** the only difference is that `~/.kimi-code` is readable, same as before the change.\n\n**Decision for you:** because the scratch `CODEX_HOME` starts empty, this lane doesn't use your `~/.codex/config.toml`. If the plain `codex` lane relies on settings there (model, profiles, MCP servers), the two lanes won't match. The fix is to copy the config into the scratch folder or pass those settings on the command line.\n\nFor the first real run, use `./run.sh fizz codex-sandboxed`. Check that `final` in `runs/fizz-codex-sandboxed-r1/answer.json` isn't empty and that `agent.err` contains no `Operation not permitted`.\n\nI also added the lane to `README.md`, and wrote the details up in `ANSWER.md`. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1791,"cache_read_input_tokens":31687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1791},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6977eebc-9437-4805-a8a1-2f1cf30e74ae","uuid":"28fc0dac-a585-4d91-b021-c2f948ab6309","timestamp":"2026-11-10T01:27:26.244Z","request_id":"req_011CgtNtmsQb1DD4GBgucWH6"}
